  "id": "COMPOUND:1216 - DISEASE:1932",
  "verbalization": "Sevoflurane, a commonly used anesthetic agent, is associated with the coiled-coil domain containing 33 (CCD33) through multiple pathways. This domain is implicated in various conditions, including Melanoma-Pancreatic cancer syndrome, Hypophosphatemic rickets, and Glaucoma 1, open angle, O, all of which are linked to the same molecular target. The shared involvement of CCD33 in these diverse pathologies suggests a potential mechanism by which sevoflurane may influence cellular processes, leading to the development of Macrostomia. The evidence indicates that sevoflurane may modulate CCD33 activity, which in turn could contribute to the pathogenesis of Macrostomia. The consistent association across different conditions highlights the importance of CCD33 in multiple physiological and pathological contexts, reinforcing the possibility that sevoflurane's effects on this domain may have broader implications for tissue development and function. The relationship between sevoflurane and Macrostomia is supported by the observed molecular pathways, though further research is needed to elucidate the precise mechanisms and clinical relevance.",
